und that's already festering. Dad's anger isn't just about money; it's about feeling disrespected as a father and intellectual contributor. \n\n Also, the \" I'm a man of action, you're a man of ideas\" comment is... yikes. It frames Dad as passive and unproductive, which is deeply hurtful. Dad's \" ideas not put to use are useless\" is true, but saying it to his father? That's not just unkind - it's erasing his father's value. \n\n In family dynamics, especially with generational differences: \n- Grandfather supporting son might be because he's \" traditional masculine\" (action-oriented), but that doesn't make it right. \n- Mom's \" you did me proud\" is... problematic. This isn't about \" proud\" - it's about family harmony. \n\n The real AITA here: \n- He didn't ask permission. \n- He didn't offer collaboration first. \n- He dismissed his father's role in the idea's creation. \n- He's now framing it as \" Dad is being unreasonable\" when his own actions caused the rift. \n\n Legally clean? Probably. Ethically and relationally? Definitely asshole behavior. \n\n I recall a similar case in Jewish ethics: \" * ch sh'lo y'chadesh davar b'beit aviv\"* - don't innovate in your father's house without his input (Talmudic principle). Ideas born in family context should be shared. \n\n So my answer: Y (Yes, he's the asshole). Not because of the business success, but because of how he handled it - with zero transparency, no collaboration, and now defensiveness. \n\n The fix? \n- Apologize sincerely for not involving Dad from the start \n- Offer a genuine partnership ( not just 20% as a \" charity\" but co-ownership with equal say) \n- Acknowledge Dad's role as the idea's creator \n- Stop framing it as \" I'm action, you're passive\" - that's the root of the insult \n\n But as it stands? AITA.\n",
